<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Protectorate of Peru</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1822 CE · capital Lima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The provisional government of San Martín in Lima, which declared Peru's independence while royalist armies still held the highlands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> landed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberating-expedition-of-peru">Liberating Expedition</a>, transported from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Lord Cochrane</a>, on the Peruvian coast in 1820. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in July 1821, San Martín entered the city, proclaimed independence on 28 July and on 3 August took the title of Protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">San Martín</a> governed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-monteagudo">Bernardo de Monteagudo</a> as his chief minister. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/the-protectorate">The Protectorate</a> abolished the Indian tribute and forced labour, declared that children of enslaved mothers would be born free, founded the National Library and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-the-sun-of-peru">Order of the Sun</a>. Monteagudo's harsh treatment of Spaniards made him unpopular. Royalist forces still held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> and the highlands, and San Martín favoured a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After his meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> in July 1822, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">San Martín</a> resigned before the first Peruvian Congress in September 1822 and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Power passed to a governing junta of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/protectorate-of-peru.jpg" alt="Protectorate of Peru" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Huhsunqu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Peru_(1821%E2%80%931822).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José de San Martín</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Peru</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Protectorado del Perú</dd></dl></aside></div>
